    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>You boost collagen naturally by giving your body the raw material and the
        cofactors it needs, then removing what destroys it.</strong> That means adequate
        protein (roughly 0.83g per kg of body weight per day, the ICMR figure), vitamin C
        with it, and daily sun protection, which prevents more collagen loss than any food
        can rebuild.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Protein first.</strong> Collagen is a protein. A 60kg adult needs about 50g of protein a day before any of it can be spared for skin.</li>
        <li><strong>Vitamin C is the switch.</strong> Two enzymes that stabilise the collagen helix cannot work without it. Around 100mg to 200mg daily saturates skin tissue.</li>
        <li><strong>Sunscreen beats every food on this list.</strong> Ultraviolet exposure is linked to up to 80% of visible facial ageing, and India gets a lot of it.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limit.</strong> Food gives you amino acids, not a measured collagen dose. No diet has been shown in a trial to raise skin collagen the way 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed peptides has.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

