      <h2 id="what-actually-works">What actually boosts collagen naturally?</h2>

      <p>
        Four things move the needle: enough total protein, vitamin C, adequate zinc, and
        sleep. Everything else on the usual internet list is either a rounding error or a
        way of protecting the collagen you already have rather than making new fibres.
        That distinction matters more than the food list itself.
      </p>

      <p>
        Collagen is the protein that forms the structural scaffold of your skin, tendons,
        bone matrix and blood vessels. In the dermis it is mostly Type I, arranged in dense
        bundles that give skin its firmness and its snap back when you press it. Your
        fibroblasts, the cells that manufacture it, are running a factory. A factory needs
        three separate things: raw material (amino acids from protein), working machinery
        (enzymes, which need vitamin C and zinc to function), and a working shift pattern
        (repair happens disproportionately during deep sleep).
      </p>

      <p>
        The reason "eat these seven collagen foods" advice underperforms is that it treats
        the raw material as the bottleneck. In most of the patients I see, it is not. The
        bottleneck is either a vitamin C intake that is technically adequate but nowhere
        near tissue saturation, a protein intake well under requirement, or a level of
        unprotected sun exposure that is degrading collagen faster than any diet can
        replace it. Fix those in order.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is also a baseline you cannot eat your way out of. Skin collagen density
        falls by roughly 1% per year from the mid-twenties, a finding that goes back to
        Shuster's 1975 work in the <em>British Journal of Dermatology</em> and has held up
        since. Nothing in this article stops that decline. The realistic goal is to slow
        it, support synthesis where it is rate limited, and stop actively accelerating it.
      </p>

