      <h2 id="vitamin-c">How much vitamin C do you need to make collagen?</h2>

      <p>
        Around 100mg to 200mg a day is the intake at which plasma and skin tissue levels
        approach saturation, which is well above the 40mg RDA set to prevent deficiency.
        Vitamin C is not optional for collagen synthesis in the way most nutrients are
        merely helpful. It is a required cofactor, and the relationship is dose dependent
        up to that saturation point.
      </p>

      <p>
        The biochemistry is simple enough to state plainly. Two enzymes, prolyl hydroxylase
        and lysyl hydroxylase, hydroxylate proline and lysine residues in the newly made
        collagen chain, and those hydroxylated residues are what let three chains wind into
        a stable triple helix and cross link into a strong fibre. Both enzymes need vitamin
        C to keep their iron centre in the right state. Remove it entirely and you get
        scurvy, a collagen assembly failure: bleeding gums, wounds that reopen, skin that
        bruises. Nobody reading this is scorbutic. But there is a long stretch between not
        scorbutic and saturated, and skin sits at the end of the queue for a water soluble
        vitamin the body cannot store.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pullar and colleagues reviewed this in <em>Nutrients</em> in 2017 and made a point
        worth repeating: skin concentrations of vitamin C are high in healthy young skin,
        fall with age, and fall further with photodamage and smoking. So the people who
        need the most are usually getting the least.
      </p>

      <h3>Where to get it, with Indian numbers</h3>

      <p>
        Amla is the standout, and it is not close. A single fresh amla carries roughly
        300mg to 600mg of vitamin C depending on variety and season, which is why it shows
        up in Ayurvedic skin preparations centuries before anyone could measure ascorbate.
        Guava gives about 200mg per medium fruit. One capsicum is around 100mg to 150mg.
        A large orange sits near 70mg. Lemon juice in dal is a real contribution but a
        small one, perhaps 10mg per squeeze, and heat degrades a fraction of it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two practical notes from clinic. First, vitamin C is water soluble and largely
        cleared within hours, so a single large dose is less useful than a moderate intake
        spread across the day. Second, pair it with your protein meal rather than treating
        it as a separate ritual, because synthesis needs both present. If you want the
        deeper version of how peptides and cofactors interact once absorbed, the
        <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">collagen peptides article</a> covers the
        mechanism properly.
      </p>

      <h2 id="protein">Are you eating enough protein to build collagen?</h2>

      <p>
        Probably not, if you are an Indian adult eating a typical vegetarian diet. The ICMR
        recommendation is 0.83g of protein per kg of body weight per day, which is about
        50g for a 60kg adult, and a 2018 survey published in the
        <em>Indian Journal of Endocrinology and Metabolism</em> found that roughly 73% of
        surveyed urban Indians had protein deficient diets. Collagen is a protein. If total
        intake is short, skin is not where the shortfall gets absorbed last.
      </p>

      <p>
        Collagen is unusually rich in three amino acids: glycine, proline and
        hydroxyproline. Glycine alone makes up about a third of every collagen chain. Your
        body can synthesise glycine and proline, so they are not strictly essential, but
        several researchers have argued that endogenous glycine production falls short of
        what full collagen turnover demands. That argument is not settled. What is settled
        is that you need adequate total protein first.
      </p>

      <h3>What a day of Indian protein actually looks like</h3>

      <p>Approximate values for common servings:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>1 katori cooked toor or moong dal (about 150g):</strong> 5g to 6g of protein. Most people overestimate this one badly.</li>
        <li><strong>100g paneer:</strong> around 18g. The most efficient vegetarian option in an ordinary Indian kitchen.</li>
        <li><strong>100g soya chunks, dry weight:</strong> about 52g, the highest density on this list by a distance.</li>
        <li><strong>1 katori curd (150g):</strong> roughly 5g. Greek style strained curd is double that.</li>
        <li><strong>2 chapatis:</strong> about 6g combined.</li>
        <li><strong>1 egg:</strong> 6g. 100g chicken breast: about 31g.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Add up a common vegetarian day: two chapatis and a katori of dal at lunch, curd,
        a vegetable, rice and dal again at dinner. That lands somewhere near 30g to 35g.
        For a 60kg adult that is a daily shortfall of 15g to 20g, sustained for years. In
        my OPD this is the single most common finding when someone tells me their hair is
        thinning and their nails split, and it is far more likely to be the explanation
        than any exotic deficiency.
      </p>

      <p>
        The fix is boring and it works: add paneer, curd, soya, eggs or dal in a second
        serving, and stop treating protein as something only gym going people count. If
        you want the comparison with whey specifically, which does a different job,
        <a href="/pages/collagen-vs-whey-protein">that is covered here</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="zinc-sleep">Zinc, sleep and the inputs nobody markets</h2>

      <p>
        Zinc activates several enzymes involved in collagen synthesis and helps regulate
        the matrix metalloproteinases that break collagen down, so it works on both sides
        of the ledger. Sleep matters because growth hormone release and dermal repair
        cluster in deep sleep, and a 2015 study in <em>Clinical and Experimental
        Dermatology</em> found poor sleepers scored measurably worse on intrinsic skin
        ageing and recovered more slowly from UV exposure.
      </p>

      <p>
        Zinc intake tends to be low in cereal heavy vegetarian diets for a specific reason:
        phytates in wholegrains and legumes bind zinc and reduce absorption. Soaking,
        sprouting and fermenting all reduce phytate content, which is one of the quiet
        reasons idli and dosa batter is better nutrition than the same ingredients
        unfermented. Pumpkin seeds, cashews, chana and dairy are useful sources. Do not go
        chasing high dose zinc supplements on your own: sustained intake above about 40mg a
        day interferes with copper absorption.
      </p>

      <p>
        On sleep, I have stopped hedging. When a patient in her thirties tells me she is
        averaging five hours and asks why her skin looks dull despite an eight step
        routine, the routine is not the problem. Seven hours is the intervention. It is
        free, and it is the one people are least willing to accept.
      </p>

