      <h2 id="what-destroys">What destroys collagen faster than you can build it</h2>

      <p>
        Ultraviolet radiation, dietary sugar and tobacco smoke. All three degrade existing
        collagen rather than merely slowing new production, which is why they outweigh most
        of what you eat. Photoageing alone has been estimated to account for up to 80% of
        visible facial ageing.
      </p>

      <h3>Ultraviolet exposure, and the Indian version of it</h3>

      <p>
        UV activates matrix metalloproteinases, enzymes that cut collagen fibres apart. The
        damage is cumulative and it does not require a beach holiday. Most of India sits
        between roughly 8 and 35 degrees north, and UV index readings above 8 are ordinary
        for large parts of the year in Mumbai, Chennai, Hyderabad and Delhi. A commute on
        a two wheeler, a school run at 11am, a window seat by the office glass: that is
        where the exposure comes from.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two Indian specific points I repeat constantly. Deeper skin tones burn less, which
        is protective against burn but does very little against the UVA that drives dermal
        collagen breakdown and pigmentation. And almost nobody applies enough sunscreen.
        The tested application rate is 2mg per square centimetre, which works out to about
        a quarter teaspoon for the face and neck. Most people apply a third of that.
      </p>

      <h3>Sugar and glycation</h3>

      <p>
        Glycation is what happens when a sugar molecule attaches to a protein without
        enzyme control, producing advanced glycation end products. In skin, these cross
        link collagen fibres in the wrong places, making them stiff, brittle and resistant
        to normal turnover. Danby's 2010 review in <em>Clinics in Dermatology</em> laid out
        the case: chronically high blood glucose ages skin structurally, not just
        cosmetically.
      </p>

      <p>
        Which is worth pausing on if you are shopping for a collagen product. Many collagen
        drinks in the Indian market carry 8g to 12g of added sugar per serving. Taking
        collagen peptides in a sugar loaded base is a slightly absurd trade: you are
        supporting synthesis with one hand and driving glycation with the other.
      </p>

      <h3>Smoking</h3>

      <p>
        Tobacco smoke reduces dermal blood supply, depletes vitamin C, and directly
        upregulates the collagen degrading enzymes. Research published in the
        <em>Journal of Dermatological Science</em> in 2007 documented the mechanism, and
        the clinical picture has a name that predates it: smoker's face, described in the
        <em>BMJ</em> in 1985. There is no supplement that offsets this. If you smoke, that
        is the highest yield change available to you, by a wide margin.
      </p>

      <h2 id="topicals">Do collagen creams work, or only retinoids?</h2>

      <p>
        Retinoids have real evidence for increasing dermal collagen. Creams containing
        collagen itself do not, because the molecule is far too large to cross intact skin.
        This is the clearest example in skin care of a claim that sounds obvious and is
        physically wrong.
      </p>

      <p>
        The constraint is a well described one. Bos and Meinardi proposed the 500 Dalton
        rule in <em>Experimental Dermatology</em> in 2000: molecules above roughly 500
        Daltons do not meaningfully penetrate the stratum corneum. Whole collagen is around
        300,000 Daltons, which is six hundred times over the limit. A collagen cream can be
        a perfectly good moisturiser, because the protein sits on the surface and holds
        water, but it is not delivering collagen into your dermis and it never was.
      </p>

      <p>
        Retinoids are the opposite case. Kafi and colleagues, publishing in the
        <em>Archives of Dermatology</em> in 2007, showed that topical retinol increased
        procollagen production in naturally aged skin on biopsy, not just on a
        questionnaire. Prescription tretinoin has a longer and stronger evidence base
        again. The trade off is real: irritation, peeling, strict sun sensitivity, and in
        Indian skin a genuine risk of post inflammatory hyperpigmentation if you push the
        strength too fast. Start low, go slow, and never start one without daily sunscreen.
      </p>

