      <h2 id="bone-broth">Is bone broth a real collagen booster?</h2>

      <p>
        Bone broth does contain collagen derived gelatin, so the premise is not fake. The
        problem is that you have no idea how much you are getting. Analyses of homemade
        broths have found collagen content varying by an order of magnitude depending on
        bones used, simmer time, water volume and whether the bones were roasted first.
      </p>

      <p>
        Compare that with what the trial literature works with. Studies showing skin
        outcomes used a defined 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed peptides daily. A cup of broth
        might deliver anywhere from under a gram to several grams of gelatin, in an
        unhydrolysed form with a much larger average peptide size, so absorption as intact
        signalling peptides is lower again. It is a food, and a nourishing one. It is not
        a dose.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is also the obvious practical point for this audience: a large share of
        Indian households are vegetarian, and bone broth is not entering that kitchen.
        Which is one reason marine sourced collagen ends up mattering here, a topic the
        <a href="/pages/marine-collagen">marine collagen article</a> handles in detail.
      </p>

      <h2 id="when-food-is-not-enough">When food is not enough to boost collagen naturally</h2>

      <p>
        Food gives you amino acids. Hydrolysed collagen peptides give you a measured dose
        of specific, absorbable fragments that behave differently once inside you. That is
        the entire distinction, and it is the honest reason a supplement can add something
        a good diet does not.
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is the mechanism, briefly. Whole collagen swallowed as food is digested into
        individual amino acids that go wherever the body needs amino acids, with nothing
        skin specific about the process. Hydrolysed collagen is enzymatically cleaved into
        short chains, and Iwai and colleagues showed in 2005 that specific di and
        tripeptides, Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly, appear intact in human blood after ingestion.
        Those fragments appear to act as signals to fibroblasts rather than as raw
        material. You cannot replicate that with a food, because no food is hydrolysed to
        that specification.
      </p>

      <p>
        The pooled evidence is moderate rather than overwhelming. A 2021 systematic review
        and meta analysis in the <em>International Journal of Dermatology</em> covering 19
        randomised controlled trials and 1,125 participants found statistically significant
        improvements in skin hydration, elasticity and wrinkle depth against placebo.
        Effect sizes for wrinkle depth were modest, several trials were industry funded,
        and sample sizes were often small. I would rather say that plainly than oversell
        it. The full breakdown sits in
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">the collagen for skin article</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Who a supplement actually makes sense for</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>You are over 30 and vegetarian.</strong> Total protein is likely short, and there is no dietary collagen at all in a vegetarian diet.</li>
        <li><strong>You are already doing the basics.</strong> Daily sunscreen, seven hours of sleep, protein counted. A supplement is the next step, not the first one.</li>
        <li><strong>You are peri or post menopausal.</strong> Skin collagen falls sharply in the years after menopause, which is covered in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-women">collagen for women</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>You will actually take it daily for twelve weeks.</strong> Adherence predicts results better than brand does.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        And who it does not make sense for. If you are not wearing sunscreen, buy sunscreen
        first, because you are trying to fill a bucket with a hole in it. If you smoke,
        stop before you spend on anything else. If you have a fish or shellfish allergy,
        marine collagen is not an option for you at all. And if a product will not state
        its collagen content in milligrams on the label, assume the number is small enough
        that they would rather you did not see it.
      </p>

