        speak to a qualified healthcare professional before starting supplementation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How can I boost collagen naturally at home?</summary>
          <p>
            Start with adequate protein, around 0.83g per kg of body weight daily, plus
            100mg to 200mg of vitamin C from food, seven hours of sleep and daily
            sunscreen. Those four cover both sides of the equation, supporting synthesis
            and preventing breakdown. Adding foods without fixing sun exposure is the most
            common mistake.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which foods increase collagen the most?</summary>
          <p>
            No food contains collagen your body can route straight to skin, so the useful
            foods are the ones supplying protein and vitamin C. Paneer at 18g of protein
            per 100g, soya chunks at about 52g per 100g dry, amla at 300mg to 600mg of
            vitamin C per fruit and guava at roughly 200mg are the highest yield options in
            an Indian kitchen.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do collagen creams and collagen skin care actually work?</summary>
          <p>
            Not for building dermal collagen. Collagen is roughly 300,000 Daltons and
            molecules above about 500 Daltons cannot cross intact skin, so a collagen cream
            works as a surface moisturiser and nothing more. If you want a topical that
            raises collagen, a retinoid has biopsy confirmed evidence behind it.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is bone broth a good collagen booster?</summary>
          <p>
            It contains gelatin from collagen, but the dose is unstandardised and varies
            enormously with bones, simmer time and water volume. Trials showing skin
            benefits used a defined 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed peptides daily, which broth
            cannot reliably deliver. Treat it as a nourishing food rather than a measured
            collagen dose.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need collagen vitamins if my diet is already good?</summary>
          <p>
            If you are genuinely hitting your protein target, eating vitamin C rich food
            and wearing sunscreen daily, a supplement is an optional addition rather than a
            fix. It becomes more worthwhile after 30, on a vegetarian diet, or around
            menopause, when intake and demand move in opposite directions. Check that any
            product states its collagen content in milligrams before buying.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can vegetarians boost collagen naturally without any animal products?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes for the synthesis side, no for dietary collagen itself. You can hit protein
            targets with paneer, curd, soya, dal and eggs, and vitamin C and zinc are easy
            to cover from plants. There is no vegetarian source of collagen peptides, since
            all supplement collagen comes from fish, bovine or poultry material.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

